# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and checked out as LF.
#
# The drafting host is Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, and the gates run on Linux
# runners (`.github/workflows/`). Without this file a shell script or a fixture could
# reach a runner with CRLF and fail there for a reason invisible on the host that
# wrote it. `text=auto` lets git decide what is text; `eol=lf` fixes what it writes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Committed fixtures are compared byte for byte (`testdata/oracles/README.md`,
# GAP-016 byte parity). They must not be touched by any filter.
testdata/** -text
*.jsonl -text

# Sparx EA wrote these, not us: they are the evidence for what EA's XMI dialect
# actually is (`docs/architecture/uaf/exports/ea-roundtrip/README.md`), so they are
# kept byte for byte, CRLF included. Normalising them would mean citing a file EA
# did not produce. Everything the exporters generate is LF and stays under the
# rule above.
docs/architecture/uaf/exports/ea-roundtrip/** -text

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.svg text eol=lf
*.pdf binary
